\subtree[design-per-constructor-box-sizing-motivation-measured-x86-64-ryzen-9950x]{
\title{Motivation (measured, x86_64 Ryzen 9950X)}

\p{\code{\verb~|nbe-closure~} ran ~1.74× slower than Koka at \em{fewer} executed instructions — the gap is memory stalls (13× the LLC misses, IPC 2.3 vs 5.9). Padding Koka's constructors to Reussir's uniform 32-byte cells reproduced Reussir's misses (0.22M → 2.51M vs our 2.42M) and runtime (125 → 238 ms vs our 224 ms) almost exactly, so node size is causally ~the whole gap. Koka sizes each constructor individually (\code{\verb~|Var~}/\code{\verb~|VVar~} = 16 B); Reussir boxed every variant at its max-arm width (32 B), doubling the numerous leaf nodes. (Ruled out by experiment: object algebra/footprint via Koka padding, the immediate encoding, borrow inference — Koka has none — and allocation \em{volume}.)}
}

\subtree[design-per-constructor-box-sizing-what-the-feature-does]{
\title{What the feature does}

\p{A boxed \strong{fused-header} variant cell is allocated \code{\verb~|header + arm[k]~} (its own constructed tag \code{\verb~|k~}) instead of \code{\verb~|header + max(arms)~}. Field offsets never move — the payload offset comes from the variant-wide alignment, so per-arm sizing only drops the trailing padding up to the max arm. Value (inline) variants stay uniform (they embed in other layouts). \code{\verb~|nbe-closure~} allocations go from \code{\verb~|218× 32 B~} to \code{\verb~|77× 16 B + 140× 24 B + 4× 32 B~}.}

\p{Note the mimalloc bin geometry: 24 rounds up to the 32-byte bin, so the real cache win is the \strong{16-byte bin for one-word arms} (\code{\verb~|Var~}/\code{\verb~|VVar~}); the 24-byte arms don't shrink their footprint until an 8-granular bin exists (\code{\verb~|MI_MAX_ALIGN_SIZE=8~}, a separate lever). On stock mimalloc this measured as a ~1.10× win with the requested-size bookkeeping halved.}
}

\subtree[design-per-constructor-box-sizing-what-size-means-per-allocator]{
\title{What "size" means, per allocator}

\p{The shipping runtime is mimalloc behind a \code{\verb~|GlobalAlloc~} whose \code{\verb~|dealloc~} \strong{ignores the layout} — \code{\verb~|dealloc(ptr, _layout) { mi_free(ptr) }~} — \code{\verb~|mi_free~} recovers the true block from mimalloc's own metadata. So the size passed to \code{\verb~|__reussir_deallocate~} is never used on mimalloc; per-constructor sizing needs only the \em{allocation} side to get the cache win there.}

\p{The exact free/realloc size matters only for a \strong{size-strict} allocator (the wasm/talc future). To keep that path correct too, decrements track the size:}

\ul{
\li{A decrement that knows its arm statically (a destructuring match, or the tag pinned by \code{\verb~|reussir-infer-variant-tag~}) produces a token of that arm's exact size.}
\li{An \strong{unpinned} decrement of a non-uniform variant produces a \strong{dynamic} token, \code{\verb~|token<align, ?>~}. It lowers to a fat \code{\verb~|{ptr, size}~} pair: the size is read at production from the box's still-live fused tag (a per-arm switch), so free and realloc pass the exact runtime size on any allocator. A variant whose arms are all one size stays a static token.}
}

\p{No unsized \code{\verb~|dealloc~} and no set-typed token were needed: the size is always recoverable — statically at a pinned dec, or carried in the fat token at an unpinned one.}
}

\subtree[design-per-constructor-box-sizing-reuse]{
\title{Reuse}

\p{\code{\verb~|TokenReuse~} pairs a dead donor token with a construction. \code{\verb~|token<?>~} is a \strong{universal fallback donor}: it can be resized to any acceptor at runtime via \code{\verb~|token.realloc~}. Scoring tiers (\code{\verb~|hueristic~}):}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|>= 2~} — exact static match → \code{\verb~|token.ensure~} (preferred; locality-scored).}
\li{\code{\verb~|1~} — a fixed-size donor in the same allocator bin → \code{\verb~|token.realloc~}.}
\li{\code{\verb~|0~} — a dynamic \code{\verb~|token<?>~} → \code{\verb~|token.realloc~} (fallback; used only when no statically-sized donor fits).}
}

\p{\strong{Binning (\code{\verb~|sameAllocatorBin~}) is only a pairing heuristic} — it decides which donor to offer to which acceptor. It is \em{not} a lowering-correctness property, so it does not gate any lowering. \code{\verb~|token.realloc~} always lowers to \code{\verb~|__reussir_reallocate~} (a real resize) followed by an invariant-group \strong{launder} of the result: an LTO'd allocator fast path may return the identical pointer, whose stale invariant-group metadata must be stripped; the ptr-eq assume keeps value propagation across the barrier. (\code{\verb~|token.realloc~}'s result is always a static token, so the launder applies to a bare pointer.)}

\p{The reuse \code{\verb~|realloc~} is \strong{no-copy} (\pr{362}): the donor's contents are dead (the acceptor overwrites the block with a freshly constructed object, and the entry points are declared \code{\verb~|allockind("realloc,uninitialized,...")~}), so the runtime keeps the block when it already satisfies the new layout (mimalloc recovers the usable size from the pointer; a same-bin resize is pointer-identity) and otherwise does a plain free + alloc — never a copying \code{\verb~|mi_realloc~}. Only the runtime's Rust \code{\verb~|GlobalAlloc~} keeps a copying realloc, as its contract requires.}
}

\subtree[design-per-constructor-box-sizing-status-follow-ups]{
\title{Status / follow-ups}

\p{Landed: per-arm allocation, dynamic-token free, and \code{\verb~|token<?>~} fallback reuse. Sound (requested alloc/free sizes round-trip; executing gate \code{\verb~|per_ctor_box_sizing_mixed_arms.rr~} and its \code{\verb~|_fixed~} sibling cover both sizings).}

\p{\strong{Per-type, on by default.} The sizing decision is a property of the variant record type, not a whole-compilation switch: the dialect variant record carries a \code{\verb~|fixed~} flag, and the source enum opts into uniform max-arm sizing with \code{\verb~|#[repr(fixed)]~}. A non-\code{\verb~|fixed~} variant (the default) sizes per constructor. The flag threads surface → HIR → MIR → the dialect type (all roundtrip-consistent), and the token-typing consumers gate on \code{\verb~|!recordType.getFixed()~} in place of the old module attribute. The earlier \code{\verb~|reussir.per_constructor_box_sizing~} module attr and \code{\verb~|rrc --variant-box-sizing~} flag are removed. Every construction path was already per-arm-correct through the \code{\verb~|getTokenType()~} / \code{\verb~|getVariantArmAllocSize()~} single source of truth — the heap path (\pr{360}/\pr{361}), the \strong{regional} bump \code{\verb~|rc.create~} (token-driven: the cell size is the token's, never \code{\verb~|getTypeSize(rcBoxType)~}; the region is a linked bump, so per-arm just shortens each cell), and \strong{TRMC} holes (the constructor's token is threaded through unchanged). The move to a per-type flag needed no lowering change; the full integration suite is green.}

\p{Remaining: the 8-granular mimalloc bin to capture the 24-byte arms (a runtime knob, orthogonal — \code{\verb~|AllocatorBinModel.h~} is only a \em{pairing hint}, so its geometry never gates correctness); benchmark the full suite to quantify the win; generated FFI marshalling (the layout was never a plain cast, so non-uniform sizing doesn't move that goalpost).}
}
